doio.c: S_openn_setup clears PERL_MODE_MAX bytes of mode, not sizeof(a pointer).

`Zero(mode,sizeof(mode),char)` where mode is the function's `char *mode`
parameter: sizeof(mode) is the size of a pointer, not of the buffer. Both callers
pass a `char mode[PERL_MODE_MAX]` and PERL_MODE_MAX is 8, so wherever a pointer is
8 bytes the two numbers agree by coincidence and nothing goes wrong. Where a
pointer is a 16-byte capability it clears 16 bytes of an 8-byte stack array.

This is a defect in perl rather than a porting difference, so the patch is
unconditional: it is a no-op on any platform whose pointers are 8 bytes. Present
unchanged in 5.32.1, 5.36.3 and 5.38.2 -- worth reporting upstream.

Found by running scripts/smoke.pl in a domain: a capability bounds fault on a
one-byte store at offset 8 of an 8-byte object, reached at the script's first
open(). The store was in memset and the caller came from the monitor's saved
return address (S_openn_setup + 0xdc).

--- a/doio.c	2026-09-26 15:42:28.045530431 +0800
+++ b/doio.c	2026-09-26 15:42:28.052528390 +0800
@@ -442,7 +442,16 @@
     *savefd = -1;
     *savetype = IoTYPE_CLOSED;
 
-    Zero(mode,sizeof(mode),char);
+    /* PERL_MODE_MAX, not sizeof(mode): mode is a char* parameter here, so
+       sizeof(mode) is the size of a POINTER, and this cleared as many bytes as a
+       pointer is wide. Both callers pass a char[PERL_MODE_MAX], which is 8, so on
+       a machine with 8-byte pointers the two numbers agree by coincidence and the
+       bug is invisible. Where a pointer is a 16-byte capability it wrote 16 bytes
+       into an 8-byte stack array: caught here at the first byte past the end
+       (capability bounds fault, one-byte store at offset 8 of an 8-byte object, in
+       S_openn_setup, on the first open() a script does). Present unchanged in
+       5.32.1, 5.36.3 and 5.38.2. */
+    Zero(mode,PERL_MODE_MAX,char);
     PL_forkprocess = 1;		/* assume true if no fork */
 
     /* If currently open - close before we re-open */
